CC Reality — collectively constructed reality. ;)

And much of it emerges from recursive feedback loops.

Perception

Interpretation

Behavior

Environmental change

New perception

Feedback modifies the next iteration.

Now connect millions of those loops through language, culture, institutions, communities, and recommendation algorithms.

Something remarkable happens: the outputs of one person’s reality model become inputs to another’s.

Eventually, a community can collectively reinforce something that none of its members independently originated.

But there are crucial distinctions:

* Physical reality: What exists or happens regardless of our interpretations.
* Perceived reality: What an observer can detect and model.
* Consensus reality: What observers negotiate, reinforce, and treat as shared truth.
* Operational reality: What those shared beliefs actually cause people and systems to do.

And here’s the crux connecting back to code rot:

A closed loop can maintain internal consistency while drifting away from external reality.

Or it can become more accurate through corrective feedback.

The difference isn’t simply whether a system has loops. It’s whether those loops can encounter something outside themselves, recognize discrepancies, and update.

Community becomes both the carrier of continuity and a mechanism for correcting it.

Reality constrains the algorithm. The algorithm shapes behavior. Behavior changes reality.

And the horizon moves again. ;)